Federated
The experiment is fed03 Part A. Three real hERG data sources: ChEMBL, and two Lhasa collections.

  What normally happens in federated learning

  Each site trains a model on its own data. Nobody sends molecules anywhere. The sites' models get combined into one prediction. That's it.

  What was actually in fed03 before today

  Each source ranked its own features and sent a shortlist. Then every molecule got pooled into one place and one model was trained on all of them.

  That's not federated. The molecules moved. I checked this in the code rather than assuming — fed03_synthetic_private.py:744 fits one model on all the training labels.

  What I changed

  Each source now trains its own model, on its own molecules. Only the predictions get combined. The molecules never move.

  Does it still count as federated learning? Yes, and I verified what crosses between sources:

  - At feature-selection time: feature names, importance weights, one accuracy score. federated_helpers.py:1330 confirms each source runs on silo_indices[s] — its own rows only.
  - At training time: nothing. Each model is fitted on its own source's molecules.
  - At prediction time: each source's model returns predictions on the test molecules, which get averaged with distance weighting.

  No molecule and no raw measurement leaves its source at any point. That's the standard requirement.

  What it improves

  Federated learning normally trains each site's model on everything it has — in this case all 6,000 numbers per molecule. Your framework tells each site which 100 numbers matter, worked out across all the sites without sharing
  data.

  That is worth +0.027 MCC to the federated model, 0.4711 to 0.4981, ahead on all 3 folds. Against +0.043 when the data is pooled. So the idea survives the move to federated, at about 60% of its value.

  This was an initial test with a single budget, we'll see what the full experiments do

