c21, in plain terms

  The question. One representation on its own already does well. Building a full hybrid over all ten is expensive and often adds little. So: can you take that one good representation and just bolt on a handful of extra features —
  and get a real gain?

  What it does. Take saprot alone (1,000 features). Offer it a small number of candidate features from elsewhere: mutation_features (48 engineered columns). Rank the candidates by how well they explain what the base representation
  gets wrong — the out-of-fold residuals — and admit up to 20.

  The trick that makes it trustworthy. It plants two kinds of fake candidate in the pool:
  - __gaussian__ — pure noise, 48 columns. Nothing should ever select these.
  - __echo__ — near-copies of features already in the base. Selecting these is double-counting.

  Whatever gets admitted, you count how many decoys slipped through. That is the honest test — not "did the score go up" but "did it go up for a real reason".

  What it found on flab (run finished 20 minutes ago, 672 antibodies, 3 folds, spearman):

  ┌───────┬────────────┬──────────┬─────────┐
  │ model │ base alone │ + extras │  delta  │
  ├───────┼────────────┼──────────┼─────────┤
  │ rf    │ 0.5683     │ 0.5762   │ +0.0080 │
  ├───────┼────────────┼──────────┼─────────┤
  │ svm   │ 0.5716     │ 0.5830   │ +0.0113 │
  └───────┴────────────┴──────────┴─────────┘

  But read the decoy counts before believing it. Admitted per fold, rf: __echo__ 21, 11, 21 and __gaussian__ 7, 2, 1 — against only 12, 7, 18 real mutation_features. More than half of what it admitted was fake. The gain of +0.008
  is real in the sense that the number moved, but the selection is not discriminating.

  That is what the admission gate I mentioned earlier was built to fix, and it does — noise 4→0, near-copies 61→8 on the old grid. On this run the gate was not on.

